Kamalie materials and fracture (@kamalie-fracture: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @ws-kamalie-games) {
## Local integrity

A thing has spatially distributed parts and integrity; one global health bar cannot express where it breaks. A brown thin stick is a connected strip of stronger parts. A bush is a cluster of weaker parts. A hit changes the contacted region, and disconnected surviving groups become physical fragments. A broken attached stick keeps only the connected portion containing its attachment point.

Impact depends on relative motion and material values. The striking stick also loses integrity with use and contact. Central strikes distribute damage through nearby bush parts, producing more small clumps with shorter scatter distances. Glancing edge strikes mostly remove the contacted edge and send detached pieces farther in the impact direction. Unhit regions retain their integrity. This is a bounded arcade material model, not a claim of structural engineering accuracy.

## Data

Use separate definition values for part integrity, damage transfer, wear, fragment impulse and geometry. Keep remaining part integrity and velocities in run memory. A material Record can own the description and its extension the validated numeric values. Values must never be parsed from prose in a simulation tick.

## Fragment budget

The initial source limits physical objects to 192 and conserves surviving parts. When splitting cannot allocate another object, remaining disconnected parts stay in one loose debris bundle; a bundle containing disconnected parts releases its attachment. This bounded fallback is deliberately less detailed than ordinary fracture.

## Later material work

- [ ] Add material combinations with mechanically distinct strength, mass and expression.
- [ ] Tune scattering against central and glancing playtest cases.
- [x] Define deterministic behavior when a fragment budget is reached.
}
